    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build succeeds with all tests passing.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the disposable-domain blocklist so that creating (or updating) an owner with an email on the blocklist is rejected with **400 Bad Request**.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:\n\n1. Added a blocklist constant:\n```java\nprivate static final Set<String> DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS =\n    Set.of(\"mailinator.com\", \"tempmail.com\", \"guerrillamail.com\");\n```\n\n2. Extended the existing `normalizeEmail(...)` method \u2014 the single validation point for owner emails, used by both create and update \u2014 to reject blocklisted domains after syntax validation and lower-casing:\n```java\nString normalized = email.toLowerCase();\nString domain = normalized.substring(normalized.indexOf('@') + 1);\nif (DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS.contains(domain)) {\n    throw new InvalidOwnerFieldsException(List.of(\"email\"));\n}\nreturn normalized;\n```\n\n3. Added the `java.util.Set` import.\n\nThis reuses the existing `InvalidOwnerFieldsException` \u2192 `ExceptionControllerAdvice` path that already maps invalid owner fields to a 400 with an `errors` array listing `email`. The domain is compared case-insensitively (against the already lower-cased email), consistent with the rest of the email handling.\n\n`./mvnw test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
